3) Azure DevOps example (Key Vault → BuildKit secret)

# Get secret from Key Vault (masked)
- task: AzureKeyVault@2
  inputs:
    azureSubscription: '<svc-conn>'
    KeyVaultName: '<kv-name>'
    SecretsFilter: 'pip-index-url'   # the full URL with token
    RunAsPreJob: false

# Build with BuildKit; pass secret as env (never echo it!)
- script: |
    echo "Enabling BuildKit"
    export DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1
    docker buildx build \
      --secret id=pip_index_url,env=pip-index-url \
      -t myorg/myapp:$(Build.BuildId) .
  displayName: "Docker build with BuildKit secret"
  env:
    pip-index-url: $(pip-index-url)


⸻

4) If you cannot use BuildKit

You can still avoid baking secrets by prebuilding dependencies outside the image:
	1.	In CI, run pip wheel -r requirements.txt -w wheelhouse/ using the secret (not logged).
	2.	Copy wheelhouse/ into the image and pip install --no-index --find-links=wheelhouse -r requirements.txt.

Downside: larger artifacts and less reproducible builds than BuildKit secrets.

⸻

5) Extra hardening tips (Python + JFrog)
	•	Use JFrog Access Tokens (scoped to the PyPI repo, short TTL). Avoid passwords.
	•	Never use ARG/ENV for tokens—they end up in image metadata/history.
	•	Don’t print the URL/token. Avoid pip -v or echo $PIP_INDEX_URL.
	•	.dockerignore any pip.conf, .pypirc, or credentials files.
	•	Private git deps? Use --mount=type=ssh (BuildKit) instead of embedding SSH keys or tokens:

# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7
RUN --mount=type=ssh pip install -r requirements.txt

Build with: docker buildx build --ssh default=$SSH_AUTH_SOCK ...

⸻

TL;DR
	•	Turn on BuildKit and use RUN --mount=type=secret ... so creds never hit layers.
	•	Prefer multi-stage: build wheels with secret, install wheels in final stage.
	•	In Azure DevOps, fetch from Key Vault and pass as a BuildKit secret (no logging, no baking).
